---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Security — File Tunnel"
  description="The capability-based, short-lived security model behind File Tunnel."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="kicker">Security</p>
    <h1>A UUID is an address, not a key.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      File Tunnel uses short-lived, purpose-specific capabilities so learning a
      tunnel identifier is never enough to read or write files.
    </p>

    <h2>Pairing</h2>
    <p>
      The QR code carries a one-time secret in the URL fragment. Browsers do not
      send fragments in HTTP requests or referrers. The portal exchanges that
      secret once for a phone-scoped capability, then removes it from the URL.
    </p>

    <h2>Separated authority</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The desktop capability observes and downloads from one tunnel.</li>
      <li>The phone capability declares and uploads to that tunnel.</li>
      <li>WebSockets use one-time, seconds-long tickets.</li>
      <li>Object-store upload grants are restricted to one object and size budget.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Content lifecycle</h2>
    <p>
      Production deployments quarantine content until type checks and malware
      scanning pass, encrypt stored objects, and delete both objects and wrapped
      keys at expiry. Filenames are display metadata, never storage paths.
    </p>

    <h2>Report a vulnerability</h2>
    <p>
      Use GitHub Security Advisories in the affected repository. Do not include
      a live QR code, capability, filename, screenshot, or uploaded file in a
      public issue.
    </p>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
